  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 AM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nA strong ridge of high pressure will remain anchored over the \nsoutheast through Thursday. This will allow for the continuation \nof dry conditions and warming temperatures, back to above normal \nby around 10 degrees. \n\nOn Friday, the ridge begins to break down and push south as a deep \nand broad long wave trough approaches from the north. Friday should \nstart out dry, but chances of rain and storms begin to increase \nduring the afternoon hours ahead of the approaching trough and cold \nfront. The front moves into our area Friday night and remains in \nplace through Saturday, with chance to likely POPS. During this \ntime, periods of moderate to locally heavy rainfall are also \npossible as PWs max out around 1.75 to 2.00 inches. These values are \nnear the max PW values for this time of year based on BNA sounding \nclimatology. Tall skinny CAPE profiles as seen on the NAM soundings, \n0-6km shear around 20kts, and warm layer cloud depths greater than \n10k feet, are all supportive of a moderate to heavy rainfall \nenvironment. Higher POPs extend into Sunday as well as the front \nstalls just to our south. Between Friday and Sunday, several inches \nor rainfall are expected across the area. Current event total \namounts range between 2 to 3 inches, with locally higher amounts \nupwards of 4 inches possible. Isolated flooding issues are certainly \npossible, especially as we get into Sunday. \n\nThe front pushes south on Monday and cooler and drier air filter in \nfrom the north. Highs will back in the 70s and overnight lows in the \n40s/50s.",
